<DocPageMeta Title="Mapping entities to DTOs"
             Section="Concepts"
             Order="40"
             Status="DocPageStatus.Draft"
             Description="How the repository translates between entities and DTOs: the four maps ShiftMapper declares for every repository automatically, the one mapper class per project where customizations live, what a repository may still say about its maps, the two hand-written doors, and what the build and startup catch."
             Keywords="mapping,mapper,shiftmapper,ishiftentitymapper,createmap,formember,reversemap,nested,maptoview,maptolist,maptoentity,copyentity,dto,entity,sm0001,sm0047,shent001" />

<DocSection Title="Why a mapping abstraction">
    <p>
        A repository spends most of its life translating between two shapes: the
        <b>entity</b> (the row the database stores) and the <b>DTO</b> (the shape
        that crosses the wire). The framework routes all of that through a single
        interface — <code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;TEntity, TListDTO, TViewDTO&gt;</code> —
        so <code>ShiftRepository</code> has exactly one mapping path, no matter
        where the mapping itself comes from.
    </p>

    <p>
        Where it comes from, for almost every repository, is <b>ShiftMapper</b>
        (<code>ShiftSoftware.ShiftMapper</code>, a source-generated mapper that ships
        with <code>ShiftSoftware.ShiftEntity.EFCore</code>). The four maps a
        repository needs are declared for you at build time, from the repository's
        type arguments; you write mapping code only for the members convention cannot
        fill, and you write it in <b>one mapper class per project</b>.
    </p>

    <p>
        One paragraph of history, because older projects and older code comments
        still mention it: AutoMapper was the original implementation and has been
        <b>removed from the framework</b>; a ShiftEntity-specific generator
        (<code>UseGeneratedMapper</code>, <code>[ShiftEntityMapper]</code>,
        the <code>SHENGEN</code> diagnostics) briefly replaced it and is gone too.
        Consumer applications that have not migrated yet still carry one or the
        other in their own code — a statement about those codebases, not about the
        framework packages. The reason for both changes is the same: convention
        mapping decided at runtime only reported its mistakes at runtime, on
        whichever endpoint a user happened to open first. ShiftMapper decides the
        same conventions at <b>build time</b> and reports what it could not decide
        as a build diagnostic, and one startup check makes sure no entity/DTO triple
        is left uncovered.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="The interface">
    <p>The contract is four methods, plus a non-generic marker:</p>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"// Non-generic marker: lets a generic parameter be constrained to ""a ShiftEntity mapper""
// without naming the triple (e.g. TMapper on [ShiftEntityEndpointWithMapper<..., TMapper>]).
public interface IShiftEntityMapper { }

public interface IShiftEntityMapper<TEntity, TListDTO, TViewDTO> : IShiftEntityMapper
{
    TViewDTO MapToView(TEntity entity, MappingContext context = default);
    TEntity MapToEntity(TViewDTO dto, TEntity existing, MappingContext context = default);
    IQueryable<TListDTO> MapToList(IQueryable<TEntity> query, MappingContext context = default);
    void CopyEntity(TEntity source, TEntity target, MappingContext context = default);
}")" />

    <ul>
        <li><code>MapToView</code> — translate a single entity into its View+Upsert DTO. Used on get-by-id and after a save.</li>
        <li><code>MapToEntity</code> — merge an incoming DTO <b>into</b> an existing tracked entity and return it. The repository hands you the entity (an empty instance for inserts, the loaded row for updates); the method mutates and returns <code>existing</code> rather than building a detached entity.</li>
        <li><code>MapToList</code> — project an <code>IQueryable&lt;TEntity&gt;</code> into an <code>IQueryable&lt;TListDTO&gt;</code>. It has to stay a translatable member-init expression — no helper calls inside the lambda — so EF Core can turn it into a <code>SELECT</code> and pull only the columns the list DTO needs.</li>
        <li><code>CopyEntity</code> — entity-to-entity copy, used by the <code>ReloadAfterSave</code> path to merge a freshly-loaded entity (navigation properties included) back onto the tracked instance.</li>
        <li><code>MappingContext</code> — carries an <code>IServiceProvider</code> (which may be null) and the action being performed when it is known. The repository passes its <code>DbContext</code>'s application service provider, so a hand-written mapper that was never registered in DI — one you <code>new</code> inside <code>UseMapper(...)</code> — can still resolve services on demand.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        You will rarely implement this interface yourself. ShiftMapper's four
        automatic maps are adapted onto it inside the framework
        (<code>ShiftMapperEntityMapper</code>), so the repository never knows the
        difference between a hand-written mapper and the generated one.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="The default: nothing to write">
    <p>
        Closing <code>ShiftRepository&lt;DB, TEntity, TListDTO, TViewDTO&gt;</code>
        is the whole declaration. ShiftMapper's generator, compiling your Data
        project, reads the type arguments off every class that closes that base and
        declares four maps — entity → view DTO, view DTO → entity, entity → list DTO
        (a projection the database runs) and entity → entity (the copy after a save)
        — with the framework's conventions applied:
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"public class ProductCategoryRepository
    : ShiftRepository<DB, ProductCategory, ProductCategoryListDTO, ProductCategoryDTO>
{
    public ProductCategoryRepository(DB db) : base(db) { }
}")" />

    <p>
        An attribute-driven endpoint — <code>[ShiftEntityEndpoint&lt;TListDTO, TViewDTO&gt;]</code>
        or <code>[ShiftEntitySecureEndpoint&lt;...&gt;]</code> on the entity, with no
        repository class at all — declares the same four maps by itself.
    </p>

    <h3>What the conventions cover</h3>
    <p>Members are matched by name, case-insensitively. Before you write anything, the automatic maps do all of this:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <b>Scalars</b> — every numeric type, <code>bool</code>, <code>DateTime</code>,
            <code>DateTimeOffset</code>, <code>TimeSpan</code>, <code>DateOnly</code>,
            <code>TimeOnly</code>, <code>Guid</code> and enums convert to and from
            <code>string</code>; enums to and from numbers; numerics between
            themselves. Text is parsed and formatted with the <b>invariant</b>
            culture. A value that cannot be converted on a write is a <b>400</b>
            naming the request field — never a <code>0</code> or a
            <code>1970-01-01</code> quietly written to the database.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Foreign keys</b> — <code>long BrandID</code> on the entity pairs with
            <code>ShiftEntitySelectDTO Brand</code> on the DTO: <code>Value</code>
            from the key, <code>Text</code> from the navigation's display member
            (<code>[ShiftEntityKeyAndName]</code>, or a <code>Name</code> property).
            A collection navigation pairs with <code>List&lt;ShiftEntitySelectDTO&gt;</code>
            the same way. The write direction converts back; a blank select on a
            required key is a 400.
        </li>
        <li><b>Files</b> — a <code>List&lt;ShiftFileDTO&gt;</code> on the DTO pairs with the JSON column on the entity.</li>
        <li>
            <b>Collections</b> — element type and container type convert
            independently, so <code>List</code>, <code>ICollection</code>,
            <code>IEnumerable</code>, the <code>IReadOnly</code> interfaces,
            <code>HashSet</code> and arrays pair in any combination.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Children</b> — child objects and child collections (JSON-column POCOs
            and owned types included) map through automatic maps of their own, ten
            levels deep, in memory <i>and</i> inside the list projection, which EF
            turns into joins. On the write direction a child collection is rebuilt
            with new objects — the build says so (<code>SM0049</code>) where the
            children are tracked rows that need reconciling instead.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Framework-owned members</b> — <code>ID</code> is never written from a
            request; <code>Tags</code> is written by the tagging pipeline;
            <code>Revisions</code> is loaded on demand. None of them is reported as
            unmapped. <code>IsDeleted</code> and the audit columns are map payload,
            as they always were; the repository refuses <code>IsDeleted</code> on
            update.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note" Title="No flattening">
        A DTO member never reaches two levels into the entity by name alone:
        <code>CountryName</code> is <b>not</b> filled from <code>Country.Name</code>,
        and <code>Vehicle.VIN</code> does not become <code>VehicleVIN</code>. An
        unbound member is never silently invented — it is reported
        (<code>SM0001</code>) and you write it, in the mapper class below.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Customizing: one mapper class per project">
    <p>
        What convention cannot do goes in <b>one</b> ordinary ShiftMapper class in
        the Data project — not partial, no attribute, no interface, nothing to
        register. <code>RegisterShiftRepositories(...)</code> registers your Data
        assembly's generated mapper, and that mapper carries every map the class
        declares together with the automatic ones.
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"using ShiftMapper;

public class MyAppMapper : ShiftMapperBase
{
    public MyAppMapper()
    {
        // A member with no column behind it: an expression, so it runs in SQL inside the list projection.
        CreateMap<Invoice, InvoiceListDTO>()
            .ForMember(d => d.Total, opt => opt.MapFrom(i => i.InvoiceLines.Sum(l => l.Price)));

        // A flattened name, written out because flattening is not a convention.
        CreateMap<City, CityListDTO>()
            .ForMember(d => d.Country, opt => opt.MapFrom(e => e.Region != null && e.Region.Country != null
                ? e.Region.Country.Name
                : null));

        // The view map, and the write map as its reverse: ""conventions first, then my code"".
        CreateMap<ProductBrand, ProductBrandDTO>()
            .ReverseMap()
            .ForMember(e => e.InternalNotes, opt => opt.Ignore())
            .AfterMap((dto, entity) => entity.Code = entity.Code?.Trim());
    }

    // Keep a replaced map behaving exactly as the automatic one did (see ""Flattening"" below).
    protected override void ConfigureDefaults(MapOptions options) => options.Flattening = false;
}")" />

    <p>The rules, all of which the build enforces:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <b>A <code>CreateMap</code> replaces the automatic map for that pair.</b>
            The build says so with an informational note (<code>SM0047</code>). Every
            other pair of the triple stays automatic, and so do the child pairs below
            a replaced map. Declare only the pairs you customize.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Declare the write map as the reverse of the view map</b> —
            <code>CreateMap&lt;Entity, DTO&gt;()….ReverseMap()</code> — as the
            automatic one is. A DTO is a subset of its entity, so the write direction
            leaves entity-only members alone; on a reverse map those are the quiet
            <code>SM0006</code>, on a forward <code>CreateMap&lt;DTO, Entity&gt;()</code>
            each is an <code>SM0001</code> warning.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Ignore a member</b> with <code>.ForMember(d =&gt; d.X, opt =&gt; opt.Ignore())</code>
            on each map the member exists in. There is no attribute for it, on
            purpose: an ignore is a mapping decision and belongs with the map.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>One class per pair.</b> Two classes declaring the same pair is a build
            error (<code>SM0042</code>). One class per project keeps that from ever
            happening.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Write the constructor as plain statements.</b> A <code>CreateMap</code>
            behind an <code>if</code>, a loop or a ternary is a build error
            (<code>SM0035</code>): the generator bakes the shape at build time and
            cannot know which branch would run. Put the condition inside the value.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>A service a map needs</b> (the hash-id service, the current user) is
            read from <code>Services</code> <i>inside the value</i>, not injected into
            the constructor. Every class the generated mapper composes is constructed
            the first time anything is mapped, so a constructor dependency would make
            the whole mapper need a container:
            <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"var hashIds = new Lazy<IHashIdService>(() => Services.GetRequiredService<IHashIdService>());

CreateMap<Group, GroupDTO>()
    .ForMember(d => d.Departments, opt => opt.MapFrom(g => g.DepartmentIds
        .Select(id => new ShiftEntitySelectDTO { Value = hashIds.Value.Encode<DepartmentListDTO>(id) })
        .ToList()));")" />
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Flattening</b> is on for a map you declare (ShiftMapper's own default)
            and off for the automatic ones. The <code>ConfigureDefaults</code>
            override in the example turns it off for the whole class, so replacing a
            map changes nothing but the members you wrote.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Sample: <code>StockPlusPlus.Data/Mappers/StockPlusPlusMapper.cs</code> —
        ProductBrand's list <code>Code</code> and write <code>AfterMap</code>, the
        Invoice list <code>Total</code>, and one member of an attribute-driven
        endpoint's map, all in the one class. ShiftIdentity's own is
        <code>ShiftIdentity.Data/Mappers/ShiftIdentityMapper.cs</code>.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="What the repository may say: how deep">
    <p>
        <b>Nothing about mapping is written in a repository.</b> What a member maps
        from is not a repository's concern — it is the mapper class's, and a
        customization that lives in the map works everywhere the pair is mapped. The
        one thing a repository (or an entity's
        <code>IConfiguresShiftRepository.ConfigureRepository</code>) may say about
        its maps is how deep the automatic child maps nest, when the default of ten
        is too many:
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"public class InvoiceRepository : ShiftRepository<DB, Invoice, InvoiceListDTO, InvoiceDTO>
{
    public InvoiceRepository(DB db) : base(db, o =>
    {
        o.IncludeRelatedEntitiesWithFindAsync(x => x.Include(i => i.InvoiceLines));
        o.Mapping(m => m.Nested(2));   // lines and their product; nothing below that
    })
    { }
}")" />

    <p>
        A constant, written as a plain statement: the generator reads it at build
        time and bakes it into the maps this repository declares.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Two hand-written doors">
    <p>
        For the cases where a map is the wrong tool, two doors predate ShiftMapper
        and are unchanged:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <b>Override the repository's mapping methods.</b>
            <code>MapToView</code> / <code>MapToEntity</code> / <code>MapToList</code> /
            <code>CopyEntity</code> are <code>public virtual</code> on
            <code>ShiftRepository</code>. An override bypasses the resolved mapper
            entirely unless it calls <code>base</code>. Sample:
            <code>StockPlusPlus.Data/Repositories/ProductRepository.cs</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>A hand-written <code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code></b> — the
            four methods as a class of its own, plugged in with
            <code>options.UseMapper(new CountryMapper())</code> in the repository's
            builder, or on an attribute-driven endpoint with
            <code>[ShiftEntityEndpointWithMapper&lt;TListDTO, TViewDTO, CountryMapper&gt;]</code> /
            <code>[ShiftEntitySecureEndpointWithMapper&lt;...&gt;]</code>. Such a triple
            declares no automatic maps, and it needs its <b>own</b> DTO type, because
            maps are keyed by DTO type — which is why the sample's mapper-backed
            Country endpoint has <code>CountryMappedDTO</code> rather than reusing
            <code>CountryDTO</code>. Sample:
            <code>StockPlusPlus.Data/Mappers/CountryMapper.cs</code>.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning" Title="A hand-written MapToList must bind ID and IsDeleted">
        <p>
            The Web layer appends <code>.Where(x =&gt; !x.IsDeleted)</code> and
            rewrites hash-id <code>$filter</code> and <code>$orderby</code> against
            the <b>already-projected DTO</b> queryable, not against the entity
            queryable. If your projection never binds those two members, there is
            nothing for any of that to reach.
        </p>
        <p>
            On EF Core an unbound member does not quietly default — the query fails
            to translate and the request throws. A missing <code>IsDeleted</code> is
            a 500 on every list call; a missing <code>ID</code> is a 500 the first
            time anyone sorts or filters, which is the shape that reaches production,
            because the grid works until someone clicks a column header. Bind the two
            members inline (<code>MappingHelpers.MapBaseListFields</code> is an
            in-memory call and cannot help in a projection). The automatic maps bind
            both — this is a contract for hand-written mappers and repository
            overrides only.
        </p>
    </DocCallout>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning" Title="Override CopyEntity too">
        If you map by hand, remember the fourth method.
        <code>ShiftRepository.CopyEntity</code> does not fall back to
        <code>ShallowCopyTo</code> when no mapper is configured — it throws like the
        other three. <code>source.ShallowCopyTo(target)</code> is the default body
        to paste.
    </DocCallout>

    <h3>Helpers for hand-written mappers</h3>
    <p>
        Hand-writing a mapper gets tedious fast for entities with audit fields,
        foreign keys and file references, so <code>ShiftEntity.Core</code> ships a
        set of extension helpers (<code>MappingHelpers</code>) that take the
        boilerplate away while keeping the mapping explicit. The framework's own
        ShiftMapper rules use the same helpers for files and required keys, so the
        two styles produce identical values.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>dto.MapBaseFields(entity)</code> — <code>ID</code>, <code>IsDeleted</code> and the four audit fields onto a view/upsert DTO. Returns the DTO for chaining.</li>
        <li><code>dto.MapBaseListFields(entity)</code> — <code>ID</code> and <code>IsDeleted</code> onto a list DTO. <b>In-memory only</b>: never usable inside an <code>IQueryable</code> projection.</li>
        <li><code>source.CopyBaseFields(target)</code> — audit fields plus <code>IsDeleted</code>, entity to entity.</li>
        <li><code>source.ShallowCopyTo(target)</code> — cached-reflection shallow copy of every settable property <i>except</i> <code>ID</code>, <code>ReloadAfterSave</code> and <code>AuditFieldsAreSet</code>. The default body for a hand-written <code>CopyEntity</code>.</li>
        <li><code>id.ToSelectDTO(text)</code> for <code>long</code> and <code>long?</code>, and <code>selectDTO.ToForeignKey()</code> / <code>.ToNullableForeignKey()</code> back the other way — the latter throwing a 400 naming the member on a blank or non-numeric value.</li>
        <li><code>json.ToShiftFiles()</code> / <code>files.ToJsonString()</code> — between a JSON column and a <code>List&lt;ShiftFileDTO&gt;</code>.</li>
        <li>Scalars: <code>ToLong</code> / <code>ToNullableLong</code>, <code>ToGuid</code> / <code>ToNullableGuid</code>, <code>ToInvariantText</code>, <code>ToValue&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>ToNullableValue&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>ToEnum&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>ToNullableEnum&lt;T&gt;</code> — invariant culture throughout, throwing <code>ShiftEntityMappingException</code> (naming the member, the value and the target type) rather than defaulting.</li>
    </ul>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"public override ProductDTO MapToView(Product entity, MappingContext context = default) =>
    new ProductDTO
    {
        Name = entity.Name,
        Price = entity.Price,

        // Foreign key + display label in one call
        ProductCategory = entity.ProductCategoryID.ToSelectDTO(entity.ProductCategory?.Name),
        ProductBrand = entity.ProductBrandID.ToSelectDTO(entity.ProductBrand?.Name),
    }
    .MapBaseFields(entity);   // ID, IsDeleted + audit fields, fluent")" />
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="How the repository finds its mapper">
    <p>
        A mapper is keyed by the <b>triple</b>: entity, list DTO, view/upsert DTO.
        Resolution happens once, in the repository's constructor, in this order —
        and it stops at the first door that answers:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <b>A mapper set on the repository options</b> — <code>UseMapper(mapper)</code>
            in the builder the repository passes to <code>base(db, o =&gt; ...)</code>,
            or from the entity's <code>IConfiguresShiftRepository.ConfigureRepository</code>
            when no builder is passed. <code>UseMapper(null)</code> also counts as
            "configured" and wins over everything below.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>An <code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code> registered in DI</b> —
            the path a <code>[ShiftEntityEndpointWithMapper&lt;..., TMapper&gt;]</code>
            attribute uses. A resolution that is itself a <code>ShiftRepository</code>
            is ignored, since a repository implements the interface and would recurse.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>ShiftMapper</b>, when the host's mapper declares all four pairs of the
            triple — the automatic maps, with whatever your mapper class replaced.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Nothing</b> — and then the four mapping methods throw
            <i>"No mapper configured. Override MapToView() or set one via UseMapper()."</i>
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        There is no convention fallback behind step 4. Above the whole chain sits
        the override door: the four methods are virtual, and an override that does
        not call <code>base</code> never consults the resolved mapper.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="The maps work anywhere">
    <p>
        The automatic maps and the ones your class declares are ordinary maps in
        your Data assembly's generated mapper, so they are not only the repository's.
        Inject <code>Mapper</code> for compile-checked typed methods, or
        <code>IMapper</code> in library code that cannot name the types:
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"public class InvoiceSummaryService(Mapper mapper, DB db)
{
    public InvoiceDTO Read(Invoice invoice) => mapper.MapToInvoiceDTO(invoice);        // or mapper.Map<InvoiceDTO>(invoice)

    public Task<List<InvoiceListDTO>> Page() =>
        db.Invoices.ProjectTo<InvoiceListDTO>(mapper).Take(50).ToListAsync();          // the same SQL projection the list endpoint runs

    public Invoice Apply(InvoiceDTO dto, Invoice row) => mapper.Map(dto, row);          // update in place
}")" />

    <p>
        A customization in your mapper class is part of the map, so <code>Total</code>
        above is summed here too, in a scope where no repository was ever constructed.
        Every project that references the Data project — API, Functions, tests — has
        the same typed methods, because a project's generated mapper carries every
        map from every referenced project.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="What the build tells you">
    <p>
        ShiftMapper reports every mapping decision it could not make, at the
        <code>CreateMap</code> or at the repository that declares the map. Promote
        any warning with <code>&lt;WarningsAsErrors&gt;SM0001&lt;/WarningsAsErrors&gt;</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>SM0001</code> <b>(warning)</b> — a destination member nothing fills: the column comes back empty, or the value never saves. Map it with <code>ForMember</code>, or <code>Ignore()</code> it.</li>
        <li><code>SM0006</code> <b>(info)</b> — an entity-only member the reverse (write) map leaves alone. The normal shape of a write map.</li>
        <li><code>SM0030</code> <b>(warning)</b> — a list-map member with no SQL form (a JSON column, an <code>AfterMap</code>): the projection cannot carry it. Move the logic to a <code>ForMember</code> that projects, or accept the member is not on the list.</li>
        <li><code>SM0047</code> <b>(info)</b> — your mapper class replaced an automatic map. Expected; it is how customizing works.</li>
        <li><code>SM0049</code> <b>(info)</b> — a write map rebuilds a child collection with new objects. If the children are tracked rows, reconcile them in <code>AfterMap</code> or in the repository's upsert.</li>
        <li><code>SM0042</code> <b>(error)</b> — two mapper classes declare the same pair.</li>
        <li><code>SM0035</code> <b>(error)</b> — a <code>CreateMap</code> or <code>m.Nested(n)</code> written where the generator cannot bake it (inside <code>if</code>, a loop, a ternary). Register unconditionally; put the condition inside the value.</li>
        <li><code>SHENT001</code> <b>(error)</b> — the one ShiftEntity analyzer: an entity declares <code>IConfiguresShiftRepository&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code> while a repository for the same triple passes an options builder to its base constructor. Passing a builder means the repository takes over, so the entity's <code>ConfigureRepository</code> would never run. Move the configuration into the builder, or drop the builder.</li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="When nothing covers a triple the app does not start">
    <p>
        <code>RegisterShiftRepositories(...)</code> wires
        <code>ShiftEntityMapperValidation.Validate</code> unconditionally — there is
        no mode or flag that turns it off. It walks every triple it can discover
        (endpoint attributes plus <code>ShiftRepository</code> subclasses) and counts
        one as covered if any of the following holds: a DI registration for
        <code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code> exists, ShiftMapper can map all
        four pairs, or the repository itself <i>declares</i> one of the four mapping
        methods.
    </p>
    <p>
        Everything it finds is reported together, in one
        <code>InvalidOperationException</code> naming each uncovered triple and the
        first things to check. In one line: this is the difference between caught in
        CI and caught in production. The same gap used to be a 500 on whichever
        endpoint a user opened first.
    </p>
</DocSection>

